1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,000
Table.

2
00:00:03,100 --> 00:00:05,300
Though maligned for its stupidity,

3
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:08,740
it survived for 11 million years.

4
00:00:08,840 --> 00:00:10,840
The happier they are, the happier I am.

5
00:00:10,940 --> 00:00:13,340
We grow it, hunt it for sport,

6
00:00:13,440 --> 00:00:15,540
and turn its innards to oil.

7
00:00:15,640 --> 00:00:18,260
It started as turkey.

8
00:00:18,360 --> 00:00:23,760
It's a pet and a poult, plucked, processed, and parted.

9
00:00:23,860 --> 00:00:24,960
What are these again?

10
00:00:25,060 --> 00:00:26,160
Turkey testicles.

11
00:00:26,260 --> 00:00:27,360
That's what I thought.

12
00:00:27,361 --> 00:00:30,360
Now, it's the turkey on Favorite Foods USA.

13
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:51,360
The turkey, an odd-looking bird from North America,

14
00:00:51,361 --> 00:00:55,360
evolved from pheasant ancestors.

15
00:00:55,361 --> 00:00:59,600
European explorers to America came upon the bird in the 1500s

16
00:00:59,601 --> 00:01:03,500
and mistakenly identified it as a type of guinea fowl called a

17
00:01:03,501 --> 00:01:07,100
turkey cock, which got its name after it was imported to Europe

18
00:01:07,101 --> 00:01:10,700
via Turkey. The cock was eventually dropped,

19
00:01:10,701 --> 00:01:14,120
and the turkey had a name that stuck.

20
00:01:14,121 --> 00:01:16,520
Whether or not it graced the pilgrim's first Thanksgiving

21
00:01:16,521 --> 00:01:20,120
is in dispute, but it's certain that Benjamin Franklin lobbied

22
00:01:20,121 --> 00:01:22,260
for it to become America's national bird.

23
00:01:25,360 --> 00:01:28,700
Still, turkeys have never been accused of being the brightest

24
00:01:28,800 --> 00:01:30,900
animals in the barn.

25
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:34,100
After all, when frightened, domesticated turkeys

26
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:38,600
will often bunch up in piles and suffocate one another.

27
00:01:38,700 --> 00:01:41,440
But there's one old story about turkey intelligence that's

28
00:01:41,540 --> 00:01:43,020
probably a myth.

29
00:01:43,120 --> 00:01:45,620
I've always heard the story told where a turkey,

30
00:01:45,720 --> 00:01:50,080
he'll just stick his head up when it rains and drowns.

31
00:01:50,180 --> 00:01:53,680
I've never seen that, 23 years of growing turkeys.

32
00:01:53,681 --> 00:01:56,480
If it starts raining, they come back in the house

33
00:01:56,580 --> 00:01:57,820
just like me and you would.

34
00:01:57,920 --> 00:01:59,820
The primary breed raised domestically

35
00:01:59,920 --> 00:02:01,580
is called the Nicholas White.

36
00:02:01,680 --> 00:02:02,680
All right.

37
00:02:02,780 --> 00:02:03,620
This is a hen.

38
00:02:03,720 --> 00:02:04,720
This is a tom.

39
00:02:04,820 --> 00:02:06,420
They're both the exact same age.

40
00:02:06,520 --> 00:02:09,720
Notable differences seen in these two sexes

41
00:02:09,820 --> 00:02:12,940
are the small, smooth profile of the hen head

42
00:02:13,040 --> 00:02:15,140
and a much smaller snood.

43
00:02:15,240 --> 00:02:17,440
That's right, a snood.

44
00:02:17,540 --> 00:02:19,980
And that fleshy thing hanging below the neck?

45
00:02:20,080 --> 00:02:21,680
That's a waddle.

46
00:02:21,780 --> 00:02:23,680
To turkeys, they're sexy.

47
00:02:23,681 --> 00:02:26,520
The waddles at the base of the neck, the snood,

48
00:02:26,620 --> 00:02:28,260
the different head coloration,

49
00:02:28,360 --> 00:02:31,460
all used to attract females for breeding in the wild.

50
00:02:31,560 --> 00:02:33,620
The eyes are offset on the side of the head

51
00:02:33,720 --> 00:02:34,860
to avoid predation.

52
00:02:34,960 --> 00:02:38,420
A turkey can typically see almost a 360-degree radius

53
00:02:38,520 --> 00:02:39,560
around them.

54
00:02:39,660 --> 00:02:42,100
A beak is basically used to procure food in the wild

55
00:02:42,200 --> 00:02:45,740
and defend itself against, you know, other turkeys.

56
00:02:45,840 --> 00:02:48,200
Sometimes hens have been known to actually sacrifice

57
00:02:48,300 --> 00:02:50,400
themselves in the wild to protect their brood

58
00:02:50,500 --> 00:02:52,480
or their pox and offspring.

59
00:02:53,680 --> 00:02:56,980
Domesticated turkeys are raised for one primary purpose,

60
00:02:57,080 --> 00:02:59,380
to be eaten by people.

61
00:02:59,480 --> 00:03:04,420
Every year, Americans consume more than 250 million of them.

62
00:03:04,520 --> 00:03:06,420
The biggest consumer worldwide?

63
00:03:06,520 --> 00:03:09,920
Would you believe Israel?

64
00:03:10,020 --> 00:03:11,760
The process of getting them to the table

65
00:03:11,860 --> 00:03:15,340
starts at breeding farms, like the Cesar Farm in Goldsboro,

66
00:03:15,440 --> 00:03:17,140
North Carolina.

67
00:03:17,240 --> 00:03:20,640
Here, breeders have devised ways to manipulate Mother Nature

68
00:03:20,641 --> 00:03:23,680
so their hens can lay enough eggs to meet demand.

69
00:03:24,380 --> 00:03:25,880
And there's not a tom in sight.

70
00:03:26,840 --> 00:03:28,240
These birds don't mate.

71
00:03:29,480 --> 00:03:31,680
That's because since the 1960s,

72
00:03:32,060 --> 00:03:34,560
domesticated hens have been artificially inseminated.

73
00:03:34,920 --> 00:03:37,880
It's enabled us to produce much more turkey

74
00:03:37,881 --> 00:03:41,200
with much fewer resources than by natural mating.

75
00:03:41,720 --> 00:03:46,640
We can have one tom for every 20 to 25 hens now,

76
00:03:46,760 --> 00:03:47,600
natural mating.

77
00:03:47,601 --> 00:03:52,340
There would be about one tom for every four to five hens.

78
00:03:52,440 --> 00:03:54,140
In this case, the domesticated toms

79
00:03:54,240 --> 00:03:56,640
get busy at North Carolina State University,

80
00:03:56,740 --> 00:04:01,140
where their semen is manually extracted.

81
00:04:01,240 --> 00:04:03,540
The semen is then sent to the hen farm,

82
00:04:03,640 --> 00:04:07,660
put inside a plastic straw, and inserted into the hen.

83
00:04:07,760 --> 00:04:10,660
The process is repeated for each bird in the flock

84
00:04:10,760 --> 00:04:14,160
approximately once a week.

85
00:04:14,260 --> 00:04:17,600
In the wild, turkeys mate in the spring.

86
00:04:17,601 --> 00:04:20,600
The increase in daylight hours triggers the hen's natural

87
00:04:20,700 --> 00:04:22,600
laying mechanism.

88
00:04:22,700 --> 00:04:24,600
By adjusting the light in the barn,

89
00:04:24,700 --> 00:04:26,600
breeders are able to mimic nature

90
00:04:26,700 --> 00:04:30,600
and artificially trigger domestic hens into laying eggs year-round.

91
00:04:30,700 --> 00:04:32,600
The reason they're coming into egg production now

92
00:04:32,700 --> 00:04:37,600
is we've restricted them from any lot other than six hours a day

93
00:04:37,700 --> 00:04:40,600
since they were 16 weeks old.

94
00:04:40,700 --> 00:04:44,600
We get them here, we boost the lights to 14 and a half, 15 hours.

95
00:04:44,601 --> 00:04:47,600
within two weeks, they'll start producing eggs.

96
00:04:47,601 --> 00:04:49,600
To increase the turkey's egg production

97
00:04:49,601 --> 00:04:52,600
to about four a week instead of the almost 12 laid annually

98
00:04:52,601 --> 00:04:56,600
in the wild, breeders alter the hen's natural nesting instinct.

99
00:04:56,601 --> 00:04:58,600
She lays a clutch of eggs in the wild,

100
00:04:58,601 --> 00:05:00,600
and she sets on them to try to incubate them.

101
00:05:00,601 --> 00:05:02,600
Well, that's what we want to break,

102
00:05:02,601 --> 00:05:05,600
because if she starts doing that, she's going to quit laying.

103
00:05:05,601 --> 00:05:08,600
So we keep these birds moved away from the nest.

104
00:05:08,601 --> 00:05:11,600
Basically, the guys push the birds out to collect the eggs,

105
00:05:11,601 --> 00:05:14,940
to collect the eggs, allows another turkey to come in.

106
00:05:14,941 --> 00:05:17,900
After the eggs are collected, they're given a quick wash

107
00:05:17,901 --> 00:05:20,740
and sent to the turkey hatchery a few miles down the road.

108
00:05:24,880 --> 00:05:27,520
We bring the eggs in from the breeder farm

109
00:05:27,521 --> 00:05:31,360
and keep them at 62 degrees with a fairly high humidity.

110
00:05:31,361 --> 00:05:35,320
The egg will remain dormant until we actually put it

111
00:05:35,321 --> 00:05:37,100
in the incubation.

112
00:05:37,101 --> 00:05:40,120
It takes four weeks to hatch.

113
00:05:40,121 --> 00:05:44,020
For 25 days, the eggs sit inside incubators at 98 degrees

114
00:05:44,120 --> 00:05:46,520
Fahrenheit while they develop.

115
00:05:46,620 --> 00:05:48,360
Workers then remove the eggs and place them

116
00:05:48,460 --> 00:05:52,260
atop a bed of lights for a peek inside.

117
00:05:52,360 --> 00:05:56,260
This allows spotters to pull any eggs that fail to incubate.

118
00:05:56,360 --> 00:06:00,480
After about a month, we've got a look at a baby turkey, or polt,

119
00:06:00,580 --> 00:06:02,180
as it enters the world.

120
00:06:02,280 --> 00:06:07,780
The temperature and humidity are all automatically controlled.

121
00:06:07,781 --> 00:06:11,700
And what we're looking at now is going to be partially hatched polts.

122
00:06:13,080 --> 00:06:14,600
You can see them through here.

123
00:06:14,601 --> 00:06:17,320
They're not all hatched out yet.

124
00:06:21,900 --> 00:06:26,600
The polt has got a very hard, what they call a pipping tooth,

125
00:06:27,340 --> 00:06:31,000
a little slight yellow hard calcium right on the end of the beak.

126
00:06:31,320 --> 00:06:35,080
It's called a pipping tooth, and this is basically what they pip.

127
00:06:35,460 --> 00:06:36,960
They pip all the way around.

128
00:06:36,961 --> 00:06:41,840
They spin inside that shell, and then they pop the shell, pop it out.

129
00:06:42,240 --> 00:06:45,040
Here's one that has pipped all the way around the shell,

130
00:06:45,920 --> 00:06:48,760
and it's just getting ready to pop the cap up.

131
00:06:52,260 --> 00:06:53,160
There we are.

132
00:06:54,540 --> 00:06:57,460
It'll take you about an hour to look just like these,

133
00:06:57,680 --> 00:06:59,580
all nice, dry, and fluffy.

134
00:07:00,320 --> 00:07:03,000
Thus begins the life of a domesticated turkey.

135
00:07:03,740 --> 00:07:05,980
The goal from here is to keep the poults healthy

136
00:07:05,981 --> 00:07:08,880
and make them plump.

137
00:07:08,980 --> 00:07:12,780
The first stop for these poults is the Tracy Price Brooder Farm.

138
00:07:12,880 --> 00:07:17,320
The first week is the main week, from my opinion, on a turkey.

139
00:07:17,420 --> 00:07:19,820
It's just like a newborn baby.

140
00:07:19,920 --> 00:07:22,220
This little poulte right here, when he's delivered to me,

141
00:07:22,320 --> 00:07:25,640
he will weigh approximately an ounce and a half.

142
00:07:25,740 --> 00:07:29,240
And in 35 to 38 days, when he leaves here, if all goes well,

143
00:07:29,340 --> 00:07:30,740
he'll weigh at least four pounds.

144
00:07:30,741 --> 00:07:35,740
Fattening up the poults means starting them off on a diet

145
00:07:35,840 --> 00:07:37,740
consisting of 28% protein.

146
00:07:37,840 --> 00:07:40,740
It's made primarily with corn, soybean meal, vitamins,

147
00:07:40,840 --> 00:07:42,180
and minerals.

148
00:07:42,280 --> 00:07:45,180
And they will be fed this for approximately two weeks.

149
00:07:45,280 --> 00:07:47,720
At that time, the protein levels will change

150
00:07:47,820 --> 00:07:50,160
according to what the birds need with the age and the weight

151
00:07:50,260 --> 00:07:51,760
that they're growing.

152
00:07:51,860 --> 00:07:54,160
Like all birds, the turkey has an unusual way

153
00:07:54,260 --> 00:07:56,660
of processing food.

154
00:07:56,760 --> 00:07:58,660
It passes through the turkey.

155
00:07:58,661 --> 00:08:02,260
It passes through the digestive tract to the crumb,

156
00:08:02,360 --> 00:08:04,460
where muscle tissue softens it up.

157
00:08:04,560 --> 00:08:07,460
It then passes to the gizzard, where tiny stones

158
00:08:07,560 --> 00:08:09,400
that the bird has instinctively swallowed

159
00:08:09,500 --> 00:08:11,400
grind it into a pulp.

160
00:08:11,500 --> 00:08:13,800
The pulp then passes into the small intestine,

161
00:08:13,900 --> 00:08:16,820
where nutrients enter the bloodstream.

162
00:08:16,920 --> 00:08:20,320
Pults need to maintain a body temperature near 105 degrees

163
00:08:20,420 --> 00:08:21,520
Fahrenheit.

164
00:08:21,620 --> 00:08:26,160
So once again, technology fills in for nature.

165
00:08:26,161 --> 00:08:29,160
The main thing in the middle here is a brooder.

166
00:08:29,260 --> 00:08:30,660
It's a heater.

167
00:08:30,760 --> 00:08:34,660
That serves as the turkey's mother.

168
00:08:34,760 --> 00:08:37,300
That's why you see a lot of them congregated in the middle.

169
00:08:37,400 --> 00:08:38,900
They're there to feel the heat.

170
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:41,200
That makes them think that that's their mother up there

171
00:08:41,300 --> 00:08:42,920
above them.

172
00:08:43,020 --> 00:08:45,120
For the next five weeks, the poults

173
00:08:45,220 --> 00:08:47,320
will be carefully nurtured until they're strong enough

174
00:08:47,420 --> 00:08:49,120
for transfer to a finishing farm.

175
00:08:52,460 --> 00:08:53,960
Hens go to one farm.

176
00:08:53,961 --> 00:08:57,560
to another, like the Hardy Cross Creek Farm in LaGrange,

177
00:08:57,660 --> 00:08:58,960
North Carolina.

178
00:08:59,060 --> 00:09:01,960
When we walk in, they're all going to follow us.

179
00:09:02,060 --> 00:09:05,300
If you notice right now, they're going to start coming to us.

180
00:09:05,400 --> 00:09:10,420
They're kind of checking us out, and which, that's a real

181
00:09:10,520 --> 00:09:11,520
healthy sign there.

182
00:09:11,620 --> 00:09:13,720
I love to hear this gobble.

183
00:09:13,820 --> 00:09:16,920
The happier they are, the happier I am.

184
00:09:17,020 --> 00:09:20,120
This is all about making money.

185
00:09:20,220 --> 00:09:22,420
A healthy bottom line depends on keeping

186
00:09:22,421 --> 00:09:25,860
on keeping the turkeys comfortable and well-fed.

187
00:09:25,960 --> 00:09:28,560
Fans and sprinklers keep the birds cool.

188
00:09:28,660 --> 00:09:32,360
Gravity-fed dispensers keep the feed bins full.

189
00:09:32,460 --> 00:09:35,760
We have hens in domestic production that go over 25 pounds,

190
00:09:35,860 --> 00:09:39,080
and our tom's up to 46, 47 pounds sometimes.

191
00:09:39,180 --> 00:09:43,680
A wild turkey to reach 25 pounds may take four years.

192
00:09:43,780 --> 00:09:46,780
Now, we can accomplish that in about 12 to 15 weeks.

193
00:09:46,880 --> 00:09:50,080
Consumer demand for big breasts and meaty thighs

194
00:09:50,081 --> 00:09:52,880
has cost the domesticated turkey one of its powers.

195
00:09:52,980 --> 00:09:55,680
With the evolution of the high meat yields on these turkeys,

196
00:09:55,780 --> 00:09:58,080
we've lost the ability to fly because the weight is much

197
00:09:58,180 --> 00:10:01,420
greater than the wings and the hollowness of the body cavity

198
00:10:01,520 --> 00:10:03,620
would be required for flight.

199
00:10:03,720 --> 00:10:06,840
Despite the extra pounds and a life without any hens,

200
00:10:06,940 --> 00:10:10,740
the toms still have their male pride and urges.

201
00:10:10,840 --> 00:10:13,840
If you look over, over to my left here,

202
00:10:13,940 --> 00:10:16,040
you'll see this turkey over here strutting.

203
00:10:16,140 --> 00:10:17,480
Look at his snoot.

204
00:10:17,580 --> 00:10:19,140
He's hanging down.

205
00:10:19,141 --> 00:10:20,300
He's strutting.

206
00:10:20,400 --> 00:10:23,640
He's trying to intimidate another turkey over here.

207
00:10:23,740 --> 00:10:25,740
This is how he's going to attract the hen.

208
00:10:25,840 --> 00:10:30,080
This is how he puts fear in the other turkeys.

209
00:10:30,180 --> 00:10:32,880
18 to 20 weeks after birth, the turkeys are trucked

210
00:10:32,980 --> 00:10:36,560
to a processing facility, in this case, the Butterball

211
00:10:36,660 --> 00:10:38,860
processing plant in Mount Olive, North Carolina.

212
00:10:42,600 --> 00:10:44,660
Soon after their arrival, the turkeys

213
00:10:44,760 --> 00:10:46,900
are humanely slaughtered and de-feathered,

214
00:10:46,901 --> 00:10:50,100
and then cleaned with an antimicrobial solution.

215
00:10:50,200 --> 00:10:52,400
Then the turkeys are put on a shackle line

216
00:10:52,500 --> 00:10:54,100
and hoisted along to be systematically

217
00:10:54,200 --> 00:10:56,200
processed for market.

218
00:10:56,300 --> 00:10:58,840
Birds processed at this facility are all toms

219
00:10:58,940 --> 00:11:01,340
and are destined to become a variety of products,

220
00:11:01,440 --> 00:11:04,960
like deli meats, bacon, and sausage.

221
00:11:05,060 --> 00:11:06,660
In turkey processing, it's important

222
00:11:06,760 --> 00:11:08,460
that we use the whole bird.

223
00:11:08,560 --> 00:11:11,400
We use this turkey neck not only for the product

224
00:11:11,500 --> 00:11:13,400
that we put in the whole bird that you can make gravy with

225
00:11:13,500 --> 00:11:15,660
at Thanksgiving, but we also use it mostly

226
00:11:15,661 --> 00:11:17,860
as a raw material for ground turkey.

227
00:11:17,960 --> 00:11:21,560
Edible organ meat is also chilled down to 40 degrees

228
00:11:21,660 --> 00:11:27,900
and then collected here, the heart, the liver,

229
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:29,300
and the gizzard.

230
00:11:29,400 --> 00:11:33,020
One of each are then placed into a giblet pack.

231
00:11:33,120 --> 00:11:35,880
You may recognize this from your last year's Thanksgiving

232
00:11:35,980 --> 00:11:36,720
turkey.

233
00:11:36,820 --> 00:11:39,120
Hopefully, you didn't cook it in the birds.

234
00:11:39,220 --> 00:11:40,460
Actually, this is for gravy.

235
00:11:40,560 --> 00:11:42,120
Ask grandma what to do with this package.

236
00:11:42,220 --> 00:11:43,560
She'll know what to do.

237
00:11:43,660 --> 00:11:45,260
After the birds have been gutted,

238
00:11:45,261 --> 00:11:47,760
They're rehung for easy butchering.

239
00:11:47,860 --> 00:11:50,100
These are 40-pound birds, and these guys

240
00:11:50,200 --> 00:11:51,760
were hanging these all day long, so they're really

241
00:11:51,860 --> 00:11:53,260
building the muscles.

242
00:11:53,360 --> 00:11:57,600
The first cut in the butchering process is the breast cut.

243
00:11:57,700 --> 00:12:01,500
We separate the front and the back half.

244
00:12:01,600 --> 00:12:04,920
Birds then swing around to the wing line.

245
00:12:05,020 --> 00:12:07,020
This is where the wing is removed from the front half

246
00:12:07,120 --> 00:12:08,020
of the bird.

247
00:12:08,120 --> 00:12:09,820
It comes off in two sections.

248
00:12:09,920 --> 00:12:12,060
You have a two-joint wing, which is cut off

249
00:12:12,160 --> 00:12:13,760
from the far end of this line.

250
00:12:13,761 --> 00:12:16,060
And then we have a drumette that's cut off.

251
00:12:16,160 --> 00:12:18,300
The drumette is a really intricate cut.

252
00:12:18,400 --> 00:12:20,400
It takes a lot of practice to get right.

253
00:12:20,500 --> 00:12:23,100
And it takes a good sharp knife.

254
00:12:23,200 --> 00:12:27,300
The remaining turkey meat is then cut in half.

255
00:12:27,400 --> 00:12:29,740
The top portion moves to the cut-up line.

256
00:12:29,840 --> 00:12:32,940
The leg and thigh processor handles the bottom half.

257
00:12:33,040 --> 00:12:35,340
The leg processor makes four cuts,

258
00:12:35,440 --> 00:12:40,160
and five pieces of meat are retrieved from those four cuts.

259
00:12:40,260 --> 00:12:43,360
As the bird enters the machine, the back is broke.

260
00:12:43,361 --> 00:12:45,860
Two cuts are made along each side of the back,

261
00:12:45,960 --> 00:12:48,960
and then the drums are removed from the thighs,

262
00:12:49,060 --> 00:12:52,940
yielding two drums, two thighs, and one back.

263
00:12:53,040 --> 00:12:56,600
And so goes the process of repairing America's turkey,

264
00:12:56,700 --> 00:13:00,580
17 pounds per person every year.

265
00:13:00,680 --> 00:13:04,180
The process peaks at Thanksgiving.

266
00:13:04,280 --> 00:13:06,960
That's also when the phones at the Butterball Turkey Talk

267
00:13:07,060 --> 00:13:07,760
Line light up.

268
00:13:07,860 --> 00:13:09,420
Butterball Turkey Talk Line, can I help you?

269
00:13:09,520 --> 00:13:12,180
And a group of women known as the Turkey Experts

270
00:13:12,181 --> 00:13:14,580
help cooks prepare their Thanksgiving Day bird.

271
00:13:14,581 --> 00:13:15,880
An icebreaker.

272
00:13:15,881 --> 00:13:17,320
The turkey's gonna be nice.

273
00:13:17,321 --> 00:13:19,220
The majority of calls can be answered with a tip

274
00:13:19,221 --> 00:13:25,120
from the Butterball cooking mantra, called the three T's.

275
00:13:25,121 --> 00:13:28,020
The first one is thaw, which means thawed correctly,

276
00:13:28,021 --> 00:13:31,440
do it in a cold way, cold refrigerator, cold water.

277
00:13:31,441 --> 00:13:33,900
The second T is to use a thermometer,

278
00:13:33,901 --> 00:13:36,540
and you want to cook the turkey until it's 170 in the breast,

279
00:13:36,541 --> 00:13:37,840
180 in the thigh.

280
00:13:37,841 --> 00:13:41,000
And our third T is what we call our two-hour rule.

281
00:13:41,001 --> 00:13:42,800
after that turkey comes out of the oven,

282
00:13:42,900 --> 00:13:45,500
we want you to have it cut up back in your refrigerator

283
00:13:45,600 --> 00:13:48,800
getting cold for those great leftovers.

284
00:13:48,900 --> 00:13:51,240
About that second tea, it's the key

285
00:13:51,340 --> 00:13:54,240
to keeping turkey meat moist.

286
00:13:54,340 --> 00:13:55,440
But how does it work?

287
00:13:57,660 --> 00:14:01,960
Once the temperature reaches 180 degrees Fahrenheit,

288
00:14:02,060 --> 00:14:05,100
the muscle fibers begin to break up,

289
00:14:05,200 --> 00:14:09,160
causing proteins to unravel and the muscle meat to become tender.

290
00:14:09,161 --> 00:14:12,760
If the meat continues to cook, the muscle proteins coagulate

291
00:14:12,860 --> 00:14:15,060
and the meat becomes dry.

292
00:14:15,160 --> 00:14:18,060
But white meat cooks faster than dark.

293
00:14:18,160 --> 00:14:20,900
By covering the breast with foil or something similar,

294
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:23,900
the heat is shielded, allowing both white and dark meat

295
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:26,820
to cook at the same rate.

296
00:14:26,920 --> 00:14:28,820
Before the turkey ladies can talk turkey,

297
00:14:28,920 --> 00:14:31,320
they're sent to Butterball University.

298
00:14:31,420 --> 00:14:33,920
And no one graduates without first learning how to carve

299
00:14:34,020 --> 00:14:35,960
the perfect slice of breast meat.

300
00:14:36,060 --> 00:14:37,960
Carve into that.

301
00:14:37,961 --> 00:14:41,960
First, disjoint the leg and thigh and pull them apart.

302
00:14:42,960 --> 00:14:46,260
Then, make a cut right above the wing just below the breast.

303
00:14:48,440 --> 00:14:49,840
Now begin slicing.

304
00:14:50,240 --> 00:14:54,940
And as you can see, those slices will fall right off.

305
00:14:56,040 --> 00:14:58,580
Not all turkey dishes are so traditional.

306
00:14:59,080 --> 00:15:02,180
One restaurant in Indiana occasionally goes off the menu

307
00:15:02,181 --> 00:15:04,380
for some more creative dishes.

308
00:15:04,860 --> 00:15:06,120
These are turkey testicles.

309
00:15:06,121 --> 00:15:10,120
We like to just serve these and we just call them turkey fries

310
00:15:10,121 --> 00:15:12,120
and leave it at that.

311
00:15:14,120 --> 00:15:18,120
Welcome to America's Turkey Restaurant.

312
00:15:18,121 --> 00:15:21,120
In Valparaiso, Indiana.

313
00:15:21,121 --> 00:15:24,120
The Strongbow, which opened in the 1940s,

314
00:15:24,121 --> 00:15:28,120
was built across the highway from its own turkey farm.

315
00:15:28,121 --> 00:15:32,120
The farm is long gone, but the menu is still the same.

316
00:15:32,121 --> 00:15:34,120
All turkey, all the time.

317
00:15:34,121 --> 00:15:36,960
From its giant turkey wings and pot pies

318
00:15:37,060 --> 00:15:40,060
to its famous turkey schnitzel and gobbler's delight,

319
00:15:40,160 --> 00:15:41,860
the Strongbow has inspired people

320
00:15:41,960 --> 00:15:44,760
to come from all over America to sink their teeth

321
00:15:44,860 --> 00:15:47,460
into some of the most succulent turkey dishes in the land.

322
00:15:47,560 --> 00:15:49,460
It's all about the turkey.

323
00:15:49,560 --> 00:15:51,340
We're kind of a destination restaurant,

324
00:15:51,440 --> 00:15:54,380
but with everybody eating healthier,

325
00:15:54,480 --> 00:15:59,280
they tend to go for more real food or choices that

326
00:15:59,380 --> 00:16:00,780
are healthier for them.

327
00:16:00,880 --> 00:16:02,880
So turkey, of course, would fit that bill.

328
00:16:02,881 --> 00:16:06,880
The Strongbow is owned and operated by Chef Russ Adams.

329
00:16:06,980 --> 00:16:09,380
Here's where he cracks open a mystery.

330
00:16:09,480 --> 00:16:12,520
What's the difference between chicken and turkey eggs?

331
00:16:12,620 --> 00:16:15,020
With the turkey, it all starts with the egg.

332
00:16:15,120 --> 00:16:16,820
And so I've got a turkey egg here.

333
00:16:16,920 --> 00:16:18,320
You can see it's speckled.

334
00:16:18,420 --> 00:16:22,140
It's a little bit larger than a chicken egg.

335
00:16:22,240 --> 00:16:23,840
And here I've got a chicken egg.

336
00:16:23,940 --> 00:16:25,340
You can see the difference between the two.

337
00:16:25,440 --> 00:16:26,540
This one's pure white.

338
00:16:26,640 --> 00:16:28,140
This one's got the speckling on it.

339
00:16:28,240 --> 00:16:30,240
These are primarily all double-yolked eggs.

340
00:16:30,340 --> 00:16:32,340
The reason we don't use so many turkey eggs in here

341
00:16:32,341 --> 00:16:34,840
in cooking is, quite frankly, they're tough to get.

342
00:16:34,940 --> 00:16:36,980
You know, the turkeys just don't produce enough eggs

343
00:16:37,080 --> 00:16:38,180
to make it worth their while.

344
00:16:38,280 --> 00:16:40,340
They're better off to use them to produce turkeys.

345
00:16:40,440 --> 00:16:42,080
Of course, there's our double yolk.

346
00:16:42,180 --> 00:16:45,080
They would also be expensive, upwards of seven times

347
00:16:45,180 --> 00:16:46,680
the price of chicken eggs.

348
00:16:46,780 --> 00:16:49,060
That's why the egg is about the only part of the turkey

349
00:16:49,160 --> 00:16:50,500
that's not on the menu here.

350
00:16:50,600 --> 00:16:53,660
But since he's got the eggs and the frying pan is nice and hot,

351
00:16:53,760 --> 00:16:55,500
let's ask the chef to try one.

352
00:16:55,600 --> 00:16:57,260
So why don't we take it on over to the table here,

353
00:16:57,360 --> 00:17:00,260
and we'll try it and see if we've got a difference.

354
00:17:00,360 --> 00:17:01,640
That's the big key.

355
00:17:01,641 --> 00:17:04,040
Whether or not the flavor is the same.

356
00:17:04,140 --> 00:17:06,540
So far, it looks about just like a chicken egg, doesn't it?

357
00:17:13,380 --> 00:17:15,780
Tastes like an egg to me.

358
00:17:15,880 --> 00:17:17,880
It's time to spice things up a bit.

359
00:17:17,980 --> 00:17:19,700
These should help.

360
00:17:19,800 --> 00:17:22,600
Turkey testicles, anyone?

361
00:17:22,700 --> 00:17:24,500
These are quite large, actually,

362
00:17:24,600 --> 00:17:26,440
for normal turkey testicles.

363
00:17:26,540 --> 00:17:28,740
I don't know what size birds these came off of.

364
00:17:28,840 --> 00:17:31,640
Right now, they're rather jelly-like.

365
00:17:31,641 --> 00:17:36,240
But after they're cooked, they have a different texture,

366
00:17:36,340 --> 00:17:37,620
but it's not what you would think.

367
00:17:38,620 --> 00:17:39,620
OK.

368
00:17:39,720 --> 00:17:42,280
And moving on, the testicles are sliced,

369
00:17:42,380 --> 00:17:46,520
soaked in a milk bath, dipped in seasoned breadcrumbs,

370
00:17:46,620 --> 00:17:49,060
and sauteed in a pan.

371
00:17:49,160 --> 00:17:51,660
Use quite a bit of butter for this.

372
00:17:51,760 --> 00:17:53,560
I think the people who would eat turkey fries

373
00:17:53,660 --> 00:17:54,960
are probably bar goers.

374
00:17:55,060 --> 00:17:57,400
Usually after a few beers, I think it probably

375
00:17:57,500 --> 00:17:59,660
would be more apt to try them.

376
00:17:59,760 --> 00:18:01,100
I'm going to let these brown on one side,

377
00:18:01,101 --> 00:18:04,100
and then we're gonna turn it over and let them brown on the other.

378
00:18:04,200 --> 00:18:07,100
And then serve them to the brave guy with a duck shirt on.

379
00:18:14,640 --> 00:18:16,640
It's good. What are these again?

380
00:18:16,740 --> 00:18:18,960
These are turkey testicles.

381
00:18:19,060 --> 00:18:20,560
That's what I thought.

382
00:18:20,660 --> 00:18:21,760
Are they really?

383
00:18:21,860 --> 00:18:23,460
What do they taste like? What do you think?

384
00:18:23,560 --> 00:18:26,060
A little bit like popcorn shrimp.

385
00:18:26,160 --> 00:18:29,500
While our good-natured diner finishes off his plate of testicles,

386
00:18:29,501 --> 00:18:31,900
Let's move on to turkey bacon.

387
00:18:31,901 --> 00:18:34,500
Have you ever wondered how manufacturers are able to give it

388
00:18:34,501 --> 00:18:37,000
the look of pork bacon?

389
00:18:37,001 --> 00:18:40,340
Butterball let us in on the secret.

390
00:18:40,341 --> 00:18:43,640
This is the beginning of the turkey bacon process.

391
00:18:43,641 --> 00:18:49,160
What we're looking here, they are loading the grinder.

392
00:18:49,161 --> 00:18:53,060
That means the frozen wing gets dumped into this grinder.

393
00:18:53,061 --> 00:18:58,900
Then it comes out, it gets grinded to a half an inch.

394
00:18:58,901 --> 00:19:02,240
is the size of particle we are doing here.

395
00:19:02,340 --> 00:19:05,100
Once the white winged meat is ground to the optimal size,

396
00:19:05,200 --> 00:19:07,740
it needs to be cooled to the proper temperature.

397
00:19:07,840 --> 00:19:10,340
We are adding, at this point, ice

398
00:19:10,440 --> 00:19:14,440
into our ground white meat.

399
00:19:14,540 --> 00:19:18,660
It is very important in the emulsion process

400
00:19:18,760 --> 00:19:22,560
to keep the meat always cold, as cold as possible.

401
00:19:22,660 --> 00:19:25,060
That's the only way to make a good emulsion,

402
00:19:25,160 --> 00:19:27,860
to make sure that the fat particles are suspended

403
00:19:27,861 --> 00:19:30,060
into the protein.

404
00:19:30,160 --> 00:19:33,460
We're going to start blending this ice into the meat.

405
00:19:33,560 --> 00:19:36,900
Then we're going to add some water.

406
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:40,800
Salt, sugar, and smoke flavoring are added for taste.

407
00:19:40,900 --> 00:19:44,000
20 minutes later, the mixture enters the emulsifier,

408
00:19:44,100 --> 00:19:46,320
where all the ingredients are bound together.

409
00:19:46,420 --> 00:19:51,120
The white meat is getting transferred to the emulsifier.

410
00:19:51,220 --> 00:19:56,660
By this way, the emulsifier gets feed into it.

411
00:19:56,661 --> 00:20:05,900
Once the meat is in the emulsifier, what we're doing is reducing the meat particles to about 1.2 millimeters in size.

412
00:20:05,901 --> 00:20:15,020
Now it's just a smooth paste that is going to replicate what will be the fat portion of regular pork bacon.

413
00:20:16,080 --> 00:20:20,860
Meanwhile, dark meat from turkey drumsticks has been mixed separately in another area of the plant.

414
00:20:21,540 --> 00:20:24,700
What we have here is larger pieces of meat.

415
00:20:24,701 --> 00:20:27,340
Your white face, you want it to be very fine,

416
00:20:28,440 --> 00:20:31,240
but the dark meat is the one that is going to be giving you

417
00:20:31,241 --> 00:20:33,500
the texture and the bite of the bacon.

418
00:20:34,600 --> 00:20:37,740
Put the dark and white meat together and...

419
00:20:37,741 --> 00:20:42,120
The secret for making the turkey bacon look like pork bacon.

420
00:20:42,960 --> 00:20:46,820
What we do here is we combine the white face of the turkey,

421
00:20:47,320 --> 00:20:51,780
this is an emulsion, with a dark mix of the turkey.

422
00:20:51,781 --> 00:20:55,880
It will both come together and make that characteristic marbling

423
00:20:55,980 --> 00:20:57,480
that you see in the turkey bacon.

424
00:21:00,720 --> 00:21:07,820
Just cook, slice, package, and off it goes to the grocery

425
00:21:07,920 --> 00:21:08,640
store bacon shelf.

426
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:14,300
Compared to an average slice of pork bacon,

427
00:21:14,400 --> 00:21:20,340
turkey bacon has 65% less fat and 10 fewer calories.

428
00:21:20,341 --> 00:21:22,580
How about turkey burgers?

429
00:21:22,680 --> 00:21:24,580
Much of the ground turkey comes from the meat

430
00:21:24,680 --> 00:21:27,540
on the turkey drumstick.

431
00:21:27,640 --> 00:21:29,540
This is our drum machine.

432
00:21:29,640 --> 00:21:33,680
This machine takes a drum hole, squeezes the meat together,

433
00:21:33,780 --> 00:21:37,220
has a piston that pushes the bone out the bottom of the drum

434
00:21:37,320 --> 00:21:39,220
machine.

435
00:21:39,320 --> 00:21:43,400
The bone exits the bottom of the off-ball,

436
00:21:43,500 --> 00:21:46,540
and the meat comes out the back and makes a premium ground

437
00:21:46,640 --> 00:21:48,840
turkey product.

438
00:21:48,841 --> 00:21:51,940
The drum meat is ground up with up to 40% of other cuts,

439
00:21:52,180 --> 00:21:54,680
depending on the fat percentage of the final product to be.

440
00:21:55,340 --> 00:21:58,240
Even though the majority of our product comes to us

441
00:21:58,241 --> 00:22:01,520
pretty much bone-free, at this stage of the process,

442
00:22:01,660 --> 00:22:03,380
we go through a secondary bone removal,

443
00:22:03,680 --> 00:22:05,860
so any tendon or any other tough tissue

444
00:22:05,861 --> 00:22:07,500
that might not be appetizing

445
00:22:07,501 --> 00:22:10,320
comes through a bone removal process.

446
00:22:10,800 --> 00:22:11,800
That's what you see here.

447
00:22:12,660 --> 00:22:15,060
Grown turkey just might end up in turkey burgers

448
00:22:15,061 --> 00:22:17,140
like those served back at the Strongbow Inn.

449
00:22:17,141 --> 00:22:20,640
And we top it with sauteed onions and shiitake mushrooms.

450
00:22:24,540 --> 00:22:27,140
And there you have our double turkey burger.

451
00:22:27,240 --> 00:22:30,180
From poulte to plate in less than five months,

452
00:22:30,280 --> 00:22:33,080
courtesy of the American turkey industry.

453
00:22:33,180 --> 00:22:35,700
While the domesticated turkey has been raised to fit man's

454
00:22:35,800 --> 00:22:40,200
needs, the wild turkey hasn't changed for millions of years.

455
00:22:40,300 --> 00:22:43,200
But if you want to eat one, you'll have to call it first.

456
00:22:47,140 --> 00:22:51,140
It's mating season in the Montana woods.

457
00:22:51,141 --> 00:22:54,140
The gobblers are getting frisky.

458
00:22:54,141 --> 00:22:56,140
The gobblers want to get with the hens and vice versa,

459
00:22:56,141 --> 00:22:59,140
so the gobbler will just basically follow those hens around

460
00:22:59,141 --> 00:23:03,140
and try to ward off any other gobblers.

461
00:23:03,141 --> 00:23:06,140
The gobblers strut their stuff against the competition

462
00:23:06,141 --> 00:23:09,140
in an elegant mating ritual that places a premium on beauty

463
00:23:09,141 --> 00:23:11,140
and attitude.

464
00:23:11,141 --> 00:23:13,140
Probably one of the most beautiful sights you can see

465
00:23:13,141 --> 00:23:15,140
is when you see one of those gobblers out there,

466
00:23:15,141 --> 00:23:18,140
his head tucked back in red, white, and blue.

467
00:23:18,240 --> 00:23:21,140
And he's got that tail fan stuck out there,

468
00:23:21,240 --> 00:23:23,040
just corralling hens.

469
00:23:23,140 --> 00:23:25,140
Very pretty sight.

470
00:23:25,240 --> 00:23:28,180
These noble birds have inhabited America long before

471
00:23:28,280 --> 00:23:30,480
the domestication of animals.

472
00:23:30,580 --> 00:23:34,100
But today, mating season is also hunting season.

473
00:23:36,300 --> 00:23:39,140
While the gobblers are focused on attracting hens,

474
00:23:39,240 --> 00:23:42,740
hunters quietly move toward the mating area.

475
00:23:42,741 --> 00:23:46,040
One sudden move could mean the end of the hunt.

476
00:23:46,140 --> 00:23:51,280
Wild turkey can fly upwards, I believe, 35 miles an hour

477
00:23:51,380 --> 00:23:53,580
or more, and they take flight very quick.

478
00:23:53,680 --> 00:23:56,580
And they can also run very fast, amazingly fast.

479
00:23:56,680 --> 00:23:58,080
So they're built for speed.

480
00:23:58,180 --> 00:24:00,900
They're built for getting away.

481
00:24:01,000 --> 00:24:03,100
What we're trying to do is convince that gobbler

482
00:24:03,200 --> 00:24:06,400
that he needs to come over here and leave those hens behind.

483
00:24:06,500 --> 00:24:08,440
So that's the game right there.

484
00:24:08,540 --> 00:24:10,040
The best way to attract a gobbler

485
00:24:10,041 --> 00:24:12,040
is to sound like a hen.

486
00:24:12,041 --> 00:24:15,040
So hunters use an arsenal of man-made calls

487
00:24:15,041 --> 00:24:17,040
to lure the turkeys to them.

488
00:24:19,040 --> 00:24:22,040
What we have here is a piece of latex stretched

489
00:24:22,041 --> 00:24:26,040
between a metal horseshoe to hold it taut.

490
00:24:26,041 --> 00:24:30,040
And it actually fits up against the roof of your mouth.

491
00:24:30,041 --> 00:24:33,040
And by using tongue pressure and letting loose

492
00:24:33,041 --> 00:24:35,040
and huffing air through it, you can get a somewhat...

493
00:24:35,041 --> 00:24:37,040
Some people are better than others,

494
00:24:37,041 --> 00:24:39,740
but you can get that hen call, the yelp of the hen.

495
00:24:39,741 --> 00:24:41,140
There goes something like that.

496
00:24:57,000 --> 00:24:58,140
No response.

497
00:24:58,680 --> 00:25:01,760
The first known turkey call came from Native Americans,

498
00:25:02,620 --> 00:25:04,540
a yelper made from a hollow wing bone.

499
00:25:07,040 --> 00:25:14,040
A tube caller mimics the mating sound of a wild gobbler.

500
00:25:14,041 --> 00:25:20,040
It's designed to entice a gobbler to come in to protect its turf.

501
00:25:20,041 --> 00:25:28,040
The most widely used call is the shrieking sound from a box call.

502
00:25:28,041 --> 00:25:35,040
It's a friction type call that's rubbing the lid across the edge of the wood, has a pitch,

503
00:25:35,041 --> 00:25:40,040
has a pitch and it's a louder call, reaches out further.

504
00:25:47,280 --> 00:25:49,160
That was just kind of like an excited hand,

505
00:25:49,161 --> 00:25:51,420
like, doggone it, where are you, big boy?

506
00:25:51,421 --> 00:25:53,360
You know, I'm looking for you.

507
00:26:00,800 --> 00:26:02,640
He's making his move in a different direction

508
00:26:02,641 --> 00:26:03,640
than I'm going, so.

509
00:26:05,040 --> 00:26:10,340
He won this match, I think.

510
00:26:10,440 --> 00:26:14,140
In the early 1900s, the match was all but over.

511
00:26:14,240 --> 00:26:16,680
Centuries of habitat destruction and subsistence

512
00:26:16,780 --> 00:26:19,080
hunting had taken a devastating toll

513
00:26:19,180 --> 00:26:21,500
on the wild turkey population.

514
00:26:21,600 --> 00:26:24,300
Commercial harvesting nearly finished them off.

515
00:26:24,400 --> 00:26:25,400
There were no laws.

516
00:26:25,500 --> 00:26:28,900
I mean, hens were taken, and there wasn't any seasons.

517
00:26:29,000 --> 00:26:33,240
So they were taken all times, even all times of the year.

518
00:26:33,241 --> 00:26:36,280
By the Great Depression, just 30,000 wild turkeys

519
00:26:36,380 --> 00:26:39,580
remained scattered throughout the United States.

520
00:26:39,680 --> 00:26:43,880
The 1937 Wildlife Restoration Act changed that.

521
00:26:43,980 --> 00:26:46,280
It imposed taxes on hunting equipment

522
00:26:46,380 --> 00:26:48,920
and used them to establish conservation and habitat

523
00:26:49,020 --> 00:26:51,060
restoration programs.

524
00:26:51,160 --> 00:26:54,660
Starting in the 1950s, hunters in the National Wild Turkey

525
00:26:54,760 --> 00:26:57,560
Federation did their share too, raising millions

526
00:26:57,660 --> 00:26:59,460
of additional dollars.

527
00:26:59,560 --> 00:27:02,640
Turkey populations rebounded dramatically.

528
00:27:02,641 --> 00:27:07,040
Now, more than 7 million turkeys from five different subspecies

529
00:27:07,140 --> 00:27:10,140
roam the wilds of North America,

530
00:27:10,240 --> 00:27:12,780
and the hunt is back on.

531
00:27:12,880 --> 00:27:15,380
We're revisiting right now the golden age of wild turkey

532
00:27:15,480 --> 00:27:16,780
hunting.

533
00:27:16,880 --> 00:27:18,280
They're back.

534
00:27:18,380 --> 00:27:20,700
Most states have a one-bird limit or two-bird limit.

535
00:27:20,800 --> 00:27:22,900
The hunters are the ones that are brought to laws,

536
00:27:23,000 --> 00:27:24,400
demanded the laws to be here.

537
00:27:27,200 --> 00:27:29,100
Of course, hunters aren't the only predators

538
00:27:29,200 --> 00:27:30,900
in the forest.

539
00:27:30,901 --> 00:27:36,340
To stay safe at night, wild turkeys use the natural habitat to keep themselves out of harm's way.

540
00:27:36,780 --> 00:27:39,698
They're a roosting bird, and so they actually have a...

541
00:27:39,699 --> 00:27:41,820
many of them have a favorite roost tree.

542
00:27:42,340 --> 00:27:44,616
They feel certainly a lot safer up in the trees.

543
00:27:44,617 --> 00:27:46,560
There's predators on the ground at night.

544
00:27:47,140 --> 00:27:49,220
It's just an instinctive thing they do.

545
00:27:50,160 --> 00:27:53,840
During the day, wild turkeys venture from their nests to gather food.

546
00:27:54,880 --> 00:27:57,840
And because they're omnivores, they'll eat just about anything.

547
00:27:57,841 --> 00:28:02,840
They'll eat everything from a plant on the right here at our feet.

548
00:28:02,841 --> 00:28:05,840
You'll see many things. They'll eat the little blades of grass

549
00:28:05,841 --> 00:28:10,840
and the seeds on top. They like berries, any kind of leaf matter.

550
00:28:10,841 --> 00:28:14,840
On average, wild hens grow to about 8 pounds,

551
00:28:14,841 --> 00:28:17,840
gobblers to around 18 pounds.

552
00:28:17,841 --> 00:28:30,520
One characteristic unique to the wild turkey comes in handy during mating season.

553
00:28:30,521 --> 00:28:34,960
Mature gobblers will have spurs half-inch or better. These here are measuring about

554
00:28:34,961 --> 00:28:37,181
three-quarters of an inch.

555
00:28:37,182 --> 00:28:42,220
It's a defensive tool, because they do fight for the rights

556
00:28:42,221 --> 00:28:46,220
for the hens. They also have here is his beard.

557
00:28:46,221 --> 00:28:50,220
It's a... Actually, it's a type of feather, although it looks

558
00:28:50,221 --> 00:28:54,220
like a hair that grows out of his chest. The beard has no

559
00:28:54,221 --> 00:28:58,220
significance that we know of. Many people look at these as

560
00:28:58,221 --> 00:29:02,220
a trophy part of a wild turkey. Native Americans prize

561
00:29:02,221 --> 00:29:06,220
wild turkey feathers for their beauty and spiritual significance.

562
00:29:06,221 --> 00:29:10,220
We do pluck many of these birds, and we send them off to Native

563
00:29:10,221 --> 00:29:12,560
and we send them off to Native Americans for ceremonial

564
00:29:12,660 --> 00:29:14,460
programs and dances and that type of thing.

565
00:29:14,560 --> 00:29:16,520
They're sought after by the Native Americans.

566
00:29:16,620 --> 00:29:18,460
And we try to comply with the wishes there

567
00:29:18,560 --> 00:29:20,260
and use as much as we can.

568
00:29:20,360 --> 00:29:22,260
The meat, of course, is delicious.

569
00:29:22,360 --> 00:29:25,260
And we use that ourselves.

570
00:29:25,360 --> 00:29:27,100
And in case you're wondering how it tastes

571
00:29:27,200 --> 00:29:28,880
compared to a domestic.

572
00:29:28,980 --> 00:29:30,780
I personally like it better.

573
00:29:30,880 --> 00:29:33,780
It's not as dry.

574
00:29:33,880 --> 00:29:36,780
It's excellent table fare.

575
00:29:36,781 --> 00:29:41,780
And this here is also excellent eating.

576
00:29:43,780 --> 00:29:45,780
Inside the gizzard are the remnants

577
00:29:45,781 --> 00:29:47,780
from the turkey's last meal.

578
00:29:47,781 --> 00:29:50,780
Remember those rocks we told you about earlier?

579
00:29:50,781 --> 00:29:52,780
Well, here's the proof.

580
00:29:52,781 --> 00:29:57,780
These are actually aids for them to help digest their food.

581
00:29:57,781 --> 00:29:59,780
The rocks are eaten.

582
00:29:59,781 --> 00:30:03,780
It's a natural thing just by their scratching, foraging bird.

583
00:30:03,781 --> 00:30:07,780
and of course they're going to pick up rocks naturally, no doubt.

584
00:30:07,781 --> 00:30:11,780
If you're going to eat the gizzard, which there's no reason why you wouldn't want to eat the gizzard,

585
00:30:11,781 --> 00:30:15,780
you actually will skin the inner wall of the stomach

586
00:30:15,781 --> 00:30:19,780
away from the meat.

587
00:30:19,781 --> 00:30:23,780
And it's not a hard process at all.

588
00:30:23,781 --> 00:30:27,780
And you have a pretty nice clean gizzard after some washing.

589
00:30:27,781 --> 00:30:31,780
National Wild Turkey Federation, our volunteers, spent a lot of time and effort

590
00:30:31,781 --> 00:30:34,420
to help bring back this beautiful bird.

591
00:30:34,520 --> 00:30:37,880
But we also enjoy the hunt and the harvest.

592
00:30:37,980 --> 00:30:43,420
And we eat the bird that we so much like to hunt.

593
00:30:43,520 --> 00:30:45,420
Nothing really is wasted.

594
00:30:45,520 --> 00:30:47,820
With hunters leading the way in conservation,

595
00:30:47,920 --> 00:30:52,040
the future of these beautiful wild birds is secured.

596
00:30:52,140 --> 00:30:54,440
But their numbers will always pale in comparison

597
00:30:54,540 --> 00:30:57,880
to the millions of domesticated turkeys raised each year,

598
00:30:57,980 --> 00:31:00,680
numbers so large that even their byproducts

599
00:31:00,681 --> 00:31:02,680
byproducts are an industry.

600
00:31:02,681 --> 00:31:04,280
It's like a snowstorm in here.

601
00:31:08,280 --> 00:31:11,160
Nearly every day of the week, Changing World

602
00:31:13,640 --> 00:31:17,800
receives 250 tons of turkey byproducts inside this harbor.

603
00:31:20,360 --> 00:31:23,400
Changing World Technologies is part of a growing industry

604
00:31:23,401 --> 00:31:26,520
geared to transforming these byproducts into useful goods.

605
00:31:27,160 --> 00:31:30,280
In this case, fuel oil, the same stuff that's pulled from the

606
00:31:30,281 --> 00:31:34,120
earth and fertilizer. As you can see in here, we have

607
00:31:34,121 --> 00:31:38,551
a mixture of all kinds of feedstock that we get in.

608
00:31:38,552 --> 00:31:42,200
This leading edge here is mostly feathers.

609
00:31:42,840 --> 00:31:49,283
The things that look like balloons are a combination of intestines and lungs.

610
00:31:49,284 --> 00:31:50,120
Of course,

611
00:31:50,121 --> 00:31:55,640
you have the feet, the beaks. The byproducts follow a conveyor

612
00:31:55,641 --> 00:31:59,740
to a set of rotating knives that chop the parts into a slurry,

613
00:31:59,840 --> 00:32:03,640
which is then pumped into a 300 degree Fahrenheit tank.

614
00:32:03,740 --> 00:32:05,480
Here, the solids, like bones and beaks,

615
00:32:05,580 --> 00:32:07,580
are separated from the liquids.

616
00:32:07,680 --> 00:32:10,580
The bone is heavier than the liquid,

617
00:32:10,680 --> 00:32:13,500
so it falls to the bottom of the tank.

618
00:32:13,600 --> 00:32:18,000
We pull that bone material off in a real rough cut.

619
00:32:18,001 --> 00:32:23,000
cut.

620
00:32:23,100 --> 00:32:28,000
It's nothing more than the bones with the marrow pulled out.

621
00:32:28,100 --> 00:32:32,440
You can see it's kind of a brownish gray.

622
00:32:32,540 --> 00:32:35,160
Looks kind of like a silly putty.

623
00:32:35,260 --> 00:32:38,060
Loaded with phosphate, the pasty material

624
00:32:38,160 --> 00:32:39,060
is sold as a fertilizer.

625
00:32:42,260 --> 00:32:45,300
Changing World Technologies also processes crude oil

626
00:32:45,301 --> 00:32:48,660
pressurized set of pipes, much like what the Earth did to the

627
00:32:48,661 --> 00:32:51,940
dinosaurs, except thousands of times faster.

628
00:32:51,941 --> 00:32:55,940
It's called thermal conversion processing.

629
00:32:55,941 --> 00:32:59,940
Pipes pump the slurry to a 500-degree Fahrenheit reactor.

630
00:32:59,941 --> 00:33:04,660
The pressure in the reactor is set to 700 pounds.

631
00:33:04,661 --> 00:33:08,060
For the next 30 minutes, the complex long-chain hydrogen

632
00:33:08,061 --> 00:33:11,860
and carbon molecules laced with minerals break down into shorter

633
00:33:11,861 --> 00:33:15,460
molecules that are identical to those found in diesel fuel oil.

634
00:33:18,760 --> 00:33:21,800
Once the pressure is lowered, pipes direct the slurry

635
00:33:21,900 --> 00:33:24,400
into a centrifuge, where the oil is separated

636
00:33:24,500 --> 00:33:27,200
from any remaining water.

637
00:33:27,300 --> 00:33:34,920
What you see here is the finished oil product

638
00:33:35,020 --> 00:33:37,820
as it's being separated away from the water.

639
00:33:37,821 --> 00:33:41,320
from the water.

640
00:33:41,321 --> 00:33:43,120
The Changing World Technologies plant

641
00:33:43,121 --> 00:33:46,760
is now at near capacity, pumping out over 7 million gallons

642
00:33:46,761 --> 00:33:50,440
of diesel fuel a year.

643
00:33:50,441 --> 00:33:53,760
And the potential for more is staggering.

644
00:33:53,761 --> 00:33:56,400
If factories processed poultry byproducts,

645
00:33:56,401 --> 00:33:59,800
including the 1.5 million tons of turkey byproducts now

646
00:33:59,801 --> 00:34:03,320
discarded each year, they could produce 30 million gallons

647
00:34:03,321 --> 00:34:04,580
of fuel annually.

648
00:34:04,581 --> 00:34:06,960
That's enough to fill the United States fuel needs

649
00:34:06,961 --> 00:34:08,200
day and a half.

650
00:34:08,300 --> 00:34:12,260
It started as turkey.

651
00:34:12,360 --> 00:34:16,100
Meanwhile, turkey feathers are about to fly inside

652
00:34:16,200 --> 00:34:19,600
Philadelphia University's textile laboratory.

653
00:34:19,700 --> 00:34:23,620
These are turkey feathers.

654
00:34:23,720 --> 00:34:25,920
You can see they're relatively fine.

655
00:34:26,020 --> 00:34:28,520
What they do is they run it through basically a shredding

656
00:34:28,620 --> 00:34:31,920
unit, a grinding unit, which will remove the quill.

657
00:34:32,020 --> 00:34:34,220
The fibers themselves have then been shredded a little bit,

658
00:34:34,221 --> 00:34:37,520
bit so we end up with a very fine, soft material.

659
00:34:37,620 --> 00:34:38,760
It's very fluffy.

660
00:34:38,860 --> 00:34:43,320
In fact, we could probably use this as an absorbent material

661
00:34:43,420 --> 00:34:45,960
in diaper applications.

662
00:34:46,060 --> 00:34:47,460
But these feathers are being tested

663
00:34:47,560 --> 00:34:50,980
for another purpose, home insulation.

664
00:34:51,080 --> 00:34:53,580
Here we have our fibers coming through.

665
00:34:53,680 --> 00:34:57,080
It's being fed underneath this roll, underneath this roll,

666
00:34:57,180 --> 00:34:59,320
into this roll, which is covered up.

667
00:34:59,420 --> 00:35:03,480
We've got a 50-50 blend of turkey feather fibers

668
00:35:03,481 --> 00:35:05,480
and polyolefin fibers.

669
00:35:07,480 --> 00:35:09,980
Looks very good. It's blended through the thickness.

670
00:35:09,981 --> 00:35:12,980
We've got feathers going through the thickness.

671
00:35:12,981 --> 00:35:14,980
They're on the other side also.

672
00:35:14,981 --> 00:35:16,980
It's a little dusty.

673
00:35:16,981 --> 00:35:18,980
It's a little messy.

674
00:35:18,981 --> 00:35:20,980
That's what happens.

675
00:35:20,981 --> 00:35:22,980
Yeah, it's like a snowstorm in here.

676
00:35:25,980 --> 00:35:28,980
If I had a little bit of tar on me, I'd be feathered already.

677
00:35:30,980 --> 00:35:32,980
Here we have the end of our roll.

678
00:35:32,981 --> 00:35:35,380
What we'll do is we'll cut the paper.

679
00:35:35,480 --> 00:35:36,580
Oh, excuse me.

680
00:35:36,680 --> 00:35:39,280
And we'll take it over to bonding.

681
00:35:39,380 --> 00:35:42,180
At this point, if you look at this part right here,

682
00:35:42,280 --> 00:35:44,320
you can see that it pulls apart very easily.

683
00:35:44,420 --> 00:35:46,020
He looks like he's talking about tar and feathered.

684
00:35:46,120 --> 00:35:46,920
Wow.

685
00:35:49,020 --> 00:35:50,740
There's really nothing holding these fibers together

686
00:35:50,840 --> 00:35:52,740
except a little bit of fiber-to-fiber friction.

687
00:35:52,840 --> 00:35:54,540
That's not going to be strong enough to hold everything

688
00:35:54,640 --> 00:35:55,940
together.

689
00:35:56,040 --> 00:35:58,580
Sending the roll through a 160-degree oven

690
00:35:58,680 --> 00:36:00,880
heats up the synthetic fibers, bonding them

691
00:36:00,881 --> 00:36:04,080
to the turkey feathers. So here it is.

692
00:36:04,180 --> 00:36:07,580
You can see as I'm pulling it off the screen that it has some

693
00:36:07,680 --> 00:36:10,320
stability to it now. It actually has some strength.

694
00:36:10,420 --> 00:36:14,020
We can handle it without it falling apart.

695
00:36:14,120 --> 00:36:16,220
All we'd have to do is add a flame retardant, and then we

696
00:36:16,320 --> 00:36:19,040
would have a fabric made with turkey feathers that would be a

697
00:36:19,140 --> 00:36:23,040
great insulating product, ready for use by the consumer.

698
00:36:23,140 --> 00:36:25,980
The university's successful tests may make insulation

699
00:36:26,080 --> 00:36:30,880
manufacturers take notice. If they bite, the four billion

700
00:36:30,881 --> 00:36:34,180
turkey feathers dumped in landfills every year may find

701
00:36:34,280 --> 00:36:37,380
their way inside the walls of American homes.

702
00:36:37,480 --> 00:36:41,020
It's all thanks to the domesticated turkey.

703
00:36:41,120 --> 00:36:43,920
One California man gives thanks in a different way,

704
00:36:44,020 --> 00:36:45,820
by giving his turkeys the opportunity

705
00:36:45,920 --> 00:36:48,640
to live a long and happy life as pets.

706
00:36:48,740 --> 00:36:49,440
Isn't that right?

707
00:36:52,800 --> 00:36:55,180
Can a turkey be a good pet?

708
00:36:55,280 --> 00:36:59,240
This California man has 40 turkeys

709
00:36:59,241 --> 00:37:01,880
and a couple of dozen chickens running wild on his Malibu

710
00:37:01,980 --> 00:37:03,340
property.

711
00:37:03,440 --> 00:37:07,180
People come on the property, and they just start laughing.

712
00:37:07,280 --> 00:37:08,180
It's fun.

713
00:37:08,280 --> 00:37:10,580
I've always been fascinated with animals.

714
00:37:10,680 --> 00:37:13,380
They just bring me a lot of happiness.

715
00:37:13,480 --> 00:37:16,000
Some of the pet turkeys were rescued from shelters.

716
00:37:16,100 --> 00:37:18,000
The rest were born here, and will

717
00:37:18,100 --> 00:37:20,100
live their entire lives foraging in the grass

718
00:37:20,200 --> 00:37:22,500
and trying to stay out of the pool.

719
00:37:22,600 --> 00:37:25,300
But there's one turkey that has the run of the place.

720
00:37:25,400 --> 00:37:27,600
This is my pet turkey, Thanks.

721
00:37:27,601 --> 00:37:30,640
And I rescued her from a place that was going to sell her

722
00:37:30,740 --> 00:37:31,740
for me.

723
00:37:31,840 --> 00:37:34,100
And she had a sister named Given.

724
00:37:34,200 --> 00:37:36,000
But Given, unfortunately, died.

725
00:37:36,100 --> 00:37:37,340
So Thanks is still with me.

726
00:37:37,440 --> 00:37:39,240
And great turkey.

727
00:37:39,340 --> 00:37:40,940
She's getting a little sleepy now,

728
00:37:41,040 --> 00:37:44,360
because she could fall asleep in my lap if I pet her.

729
00:37:44,460 --> 00:37:46,260
Fortunately for Thanks, she usually

730
00:37:46,360 --> 00:37:48,360
stays awake long enough to walk around the house

731
00:37:48,460 --> 00:37:50,060
until she finds the dog food dish.

732
00:37:52,860 --> 00:37:56,560
Who doesn't have a turkey as a pet, huh?

733
00:37:56,561 --> 00:37:58,560
Come on outside. Let's go.

734
00:37:58,561 --> 00:38:00,560
Oh, here we go.

735
00:38:00,561 --> 00:38:02,560
You've had enough.

736
00:38:05,560 --> 00:38:07,560
Aside from the occasional coyote,

737
00:38:07,561 --> 00:38:10,560
the turkeys are free from predators and worry,

738
00:38:10,561 --> 00:38:13,560
giving their owner the chance to observe their behavior.

739
00:38:15,560 --> 00:38:19,560
They all have really distinct personalities,

740
00:38:19,561 --> 00:38:21,560
and they're very loud.

741
00:38:21,561 --> 00:38:35,561
I think that a lot of people see turkeys as a commodity or as meat, but I see them as these amazing creatures who have personalities, who have a spirit, and love life.

742
00:38:36,360 --> 00:38:40,260
This spring, turkey love brought another generation of turkeys to the property.

743
00:38:40,920 --> 00:38:45,920
I had a pleasant surprise. One of my turkeys hid in the yard and had 16 babies.

744
00:38:45,921 --> 00:38:52,760
So we have an addition to the family, which is, it's a lot.

745
00:38:53,420 --> 00:38:54,240
It's a lot.

746
00:38:55,500 --> 00:38:58,460
For most turkeys, the only path to this kind of freedom

747
00:38:58,461 --> 00:38:59,980
is through a presidential pardon.

748
00:39:01,040 --> 00:39:03,740
Every year, the White House stages the pre-Thanksgiving Day

749
00:39:03,741 --> 00:39:06,160
ceremony as a gesture of goodwill.

750
00:39:07,120 --> 00:39:09,040
The White House credits the Truman administration

751
00:39:09,041 --> 00:39:11,720
with starting the ritual, although some historians

752
00:39:11,721 --> 00:39:13,600
believe it dates back to Abraham Lincoln.

753
00:39:15,920 --> 00:39:19,920
It's up to specially selected breeders to raise the lucky

754
00:39:20,020 --> 00:39:20,920
turkey.

755
00:39:21,020 --> 00:39:25,260
In 2003, the task fell to Jim Stockham and Tom Fix.

756
00:39:25,360 --> 00:39:29,460
We wanted to see birds that had a bit of a personality.

757
00:39:29,560 --> 00:39:33,280
We wanted to see birds that would strut, that would gobble.

758
00:39:33,380 --> 00:39:36,600
The men settled on two birds, one for the ceremony

759
00:39:36,700 --> 00:39:38,040
and one as an alternate.

760
00:39:38,140 --> 00:39:41,520
They were named Stars and Stripes.

761
00:39:41,620 --> 00:39:44,720
Just before Thanksgiving, the men and their birds checked

762
00:39:44,721 --> 00:39:47,120
checked into the historic Hotel Washington,

763
00:39:47,220 --> 00:39:49,420
just across the street from the White House.

764
00:39:49,520 --> 00:39:51,460
That evening, we couldn't believe

765
00:39:51,560 --> 00:39:54,860
that a couple of guys from southwest Missouri

766
00:39:54,960 --> 00:39:58,760
had the entire top floor of the Hotel Washington

767
00:39:58,860 --> 00:40:00,360
as a turkey barn.

768
00:40:00,460 --> 00:40:02,280
On the big day, school children,

769
00:40:02,380 --> 00:40:05,680
turkey industry dignitaries, and a gaggle of press

